{
  "dom_id": "step-t8071",
  "name": "Vertical Lines",
  "problem": "Suppose that we want to find the equation of the **vertical line** that passes through the point $(-4,0)$, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166027/assets/065dfc149c1624bbe2c6fd51085c48b5e60c1d37d248d073dd1374d08e65b5af.png)\n\nNotice that for *any* $y$-value on the line, the $x$-value is always $\\color{red}-4.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166027/assets/01d24274cc3d2b5a7899cc1aa8cee4aef4fb02ddb45acd27629a57d6af41a398.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of this vertical line is $x=-4.$\n\n**Note**: When writing the equation of a vertical line, we always put the variable $x$ on the left side and the constant on the right side.",
